    <h3>Real-World Examples: O(n) vs O(n²)</h3>
    <p>Let's compare two approaches to finding duplicate numbers in an array:</p>

    <h4>O(n²) Approach - Nested Loops:</h4>
    <pre><code class="language-python">def find_duplicates_slow(arr):
    duplicates = []
    for i in range(len(arr)):
        for j in range(i + 1, len(arr)):
            if arr[i] == arr[j]:
                duplicates.append(arr[i])
    return duplicates</code></pre>

    <p><strong>Why O(n²)?</strong> For every element (outer loop), we check every remaining element (inner loop). With 1,000 elements, this means ~500,000 comparisons.</p>

    <h4>O(n) Approach - Hash Set:</h4>
    <pre><code class="language-python">def find_duplicates_fast(arr):
    seen = set()
    duplicates = set()
    for num in arr:
        if num in seen:
            duplicates.add(num)
        else:
            seen.add(num)
    return list(duplicates)</code></pre>

    <p><strong>Why O(n)?</strong> We iterate through the array once, using a hash set for O(1) lookups. With 1,000 elements, this means ~1,000 operations.</p>

    <p><strong>Performance Difference:</strong> On a list of 10,000 elements, the slow version performs ~50 million operations, while the fast version performs ~10,000. That's a 5,000x improvement!</p>

    <h3>How to Optimize Slow Code</h3>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Identify the Problem:</strong> Use Big-O analysis to find O(n²) and worse algorithms</li>
      <li><strong>Use Appropriate Data Structures:</strong> Hash sets for O(1) lookups, binary search trees for sorted data</li>
      <li><strong>Reduce Nested Loops:</strong> If possible, eliminate nested iterations</li>
      <li><strong>Apply Algorithms:</strong> Use sorting algorithms like merge sort O(n log n) instead of bubble sort O(n²)</li>
      <li><strong>Memoization/Caching:</strong> Store computed results to avoid redundant calculations</li>
      <li><strong>Divide and Conquer:</strong> Break problems into smaller subproblems</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Common Pitfalls to Avoid</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Assuming O(1) operations inside loops (they still contribute to overall complexity)</li>
      <li>Forgetting about constant factors in real-world performance</li>
      <li>Not considering space complexity alongside time complexity</li>
      <li>Premature optimization for cases that won't scale to large inputs</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

